Enums (short for Enumerations) are a way to declare a type with a finite set of named values. This is useful when you want to define a fixed set of constants that you can assign to variables and use in a more readable manner.

Here’s how you might declare an enum in various languages:

### C, C++, Rust

```c
// C
enum Color { RED, GREEN, BLUE };

// C++
enum class Color { RED, GREEN, BLUE };

// Rust
#[derive(Debug)]
enum Color {
    Red,
    Green,
    Blue,
}
```

